<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Russian Volunteer Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bermontians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The West Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteer-army">Volunteer Army</a> was a force formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> in 1919 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-bermondt-avalov">Pavel Bermondt-Avalov</a>, combining anti-Bolshevik Russian former prisoners of war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">German Freikorps</a> troops who had stayed in the Baltic. Nominally fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, it attacked the Latvian government at Riga in October 1919, but was driven back in November and defeated by Lithuanian forces near Radviliškis, and its remnants withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> by December.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-russian-volunteer-army.jpg" alt="West Russian Volunteer Army" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PĒTERIS CEDRIŅS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Russian_Volunteer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Article 12 of the Compiègne Armistice of November 1918 required German troops to stay in the Baltic provinces of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> to help hold back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> judged the situation under control. The Allies ordered their withdrawal after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> was signed in June 1919, but most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> stayed on under General Rüdiger von der Goltz, who kept in the background so as not to compromise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>The first units of the future army were brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> on 30 May 1919. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-bermondt-avalov">Pavel Bermondt-Avalov</a> set up his headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Jelgava</a> on 12 June and on 10 July formed the Central Council of the Western Russian District, which issued money printed in Germany and received funds and arms from German industrialists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a>. In August von der Goltz merged his troops with Bermondt-Avalov's Special Russian Corps. Of the roughly 50,000 men they recruited, most were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> members and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-germans">Baltic Germans</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The army declared its support for the White forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kolchak">Alexander Kolchak</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and set out eastward against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, but its real aim appeared to be to keep German power in the Baltic. Under Entente pressure the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> government banned the transfer of soldiers to it, closed the East Prussian border to its supplies and recalled von der Goltz on 4 October. Bermondt-Avalov then attacked, seizing the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> at Riga, so that the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a> appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> for help. Estonia sent two armoured trains, and the British destroyer HMS Vanoc supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> from Riga harbour.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> the Bermontians occupied Biržai, Radviliškis, Šiauliai, Raseiniai and other towns, attacking and looting the population, and the Lithuanian government had to pull units from the front against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. On 11 November the Latvian army defeated them near Riga. Germany took the army under its protection on 18 November, and an Entente request halted the Lithuanian pursuit after the battle. Bermondt-Avalov fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a>, and General Walter von Eberhardt organised the evacuation; the last units left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> on 1 December and Lithuania on 15 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The army wore German uniforms with Russian insignia, shoulder boards on the Imperial Russian pattern and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> cross on the left sleeve. Its largest formations were the Iron Division under Major Bischoff, about 15,000 to 18,000 men at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Jelgava</a>, the German Legion of 9,000 to 12,000, and Colonel Potozki's Corps Graf Keller of 7,000 to 10,000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Russian Volunteer Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Latvian and Estonian troops defeated the German Landeswehr at the Battle of Cēsis in June 1919, the national government returned to Riga, and the attack of Pavel Bermondt-Avalov's <b>West Russian Volunteer Army</b> on the capital was repulsed in November.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state then had to fight for its existence against Soviet Russia, the <b>Bermontians</b> and Poland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It then defeated the <b>West Russian Volunteer Army</b>, the Bermontians, some 50,000 men armed by Germany, by the end of 1919.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Military</a></li></ul></section>
